@page "/docs/accessibility/component-status"
@inject Registry Registry
@inject ApiMetadata Api

@* Every component with what has been checked on it, in three columns that mean different things: its keys (the API
   metadata's Keyboard table), the automated audit of its docs pages (site/IzzyBlazor.Site/.izzy/audit-baseline.json: remake
   the numbers in Run and the Accepted list when the baseline is remade), and screen readers
   (artifacts/a11y-audit/SCREEN-READER-PASS.md: add a component to ScreenReaders as the pass reaches it, and say whether a
   script's transcript was read or a person listened). A cell says what was done, never "conforms". After Syncfusion's
   per-component table (Nov 25, 2026), which has no column for what wasn't tested. *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="component-status">
    <Lead>
        Every component, with what has been checked on it and how. A check by a tool and a test with a screen
        reader are separate columns, since
        <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/automated-and-manual">they answer different questions</Anchor>. Where a
        test hasn't been done, the cell says so.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">What the columns mean</Heading>
        <Table Items="Meanings" Caption="The columns of the status table" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Column" Width="Length.Rem(10)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Says">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">The automated audit</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit">izzy audit</Anchor> ran over this site on @Run.On:
            @Run.Pages pages, each at 1440 and 375 pixels wide, in light and dark, and once in forced colors.
            It runs axe-core and <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-checks">four checks of its own</Anchor>
            on the docs pages of every component, as they load. The run had @Run.Findings findings, and each was judged. None is
            open: @Run.FalsePositives were false positives of a rule, @Run.DemoPages came from a docs
            page showing a component several times, and @Run.Accepted are low-contrast text in docs examples
            that show a color on purpose. Those @Run.Accepted are real and accepted, with the reason in the report.
            axe could not measure the contrast of some text, such as text over a picture or a gradient. Those
            elements are counted apart and are not passes.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            "No open findings" in the Audit column says that every finding on the component's examples was
            judged, and none was real or left for a person. It covers the examples as they load. It doesn't
            cover a closed dialog or menu, the order of the keyboard or what a screen reader says. The audit
            measures the contrast of text, not of borders and icons.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Screen readers</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A pass with screen readers has started and isn't done. Names, roles and states are checked in the
            browser's accessibility tree, which is what a screen reader reads, but that is not the same as
            listening to one. The pass covers VoiceOver with Safari on a Mac, then NVDA with Chrome and Firefox,
            JAWS with Chrome and Narrator with Edge on Windows, VoiceOver with Safari on an iPhone and TalkBack
            with Chrome on Android. A component's cell names the screen readers it has been through and how: by
            a script, whose transcript of what was spoken a person then read, or by a person listening. If a
            screen reader gets something wrong,
            <Anchor Href="https://github.com/IzzyBlazor/izzyblazor/issues" External>report it as an issue</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Known gaps</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A component with a known gap is marked in the table. The
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/overview#known-gaps">overview</Anchor> describes each gap and its
            workaround. One gap is shared by every field and switch and isn't marked on each: their borders
            have about 2:1 contrast against the page. The audit doesn't measure border contrast, so it doesn't
            report this gap.
        </TextBlock>

        @foreach (var category in Registry.Categories)
        {
            <Heading Level="2">@category</Heading>
            <Table Items="@Rows(category)" Caption="@($"{category}: accessibility status")" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose site-params-status" Bordered="false">
                <Columns>
                    <TemplateColumn Title="Component"><Anchor Href="@context!.Href">@context.Name</Anchor></TemplateColumn>
                    <TemplateColumn Title="Keyboard">
                        @if (context!.Keys)
                        {
                            <Anchor Href="@($"{context.Href}#keyboard-navigation")">Keys documented</Anchor>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <text>No keys of its own</text>
                        }
                    </TemplateColumn>
                    <TemplateColumn Title="Audit">@context!.Audit</TemplateColumn>
                    <TemplateColumn Title="Screen readers">@context!.ScreenReaders</TemplateColumn>
                </Columns>
            </Table>
        }
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private sealed record Status(string Name, string Href, bool Keys, string Audit, string ScreenReaders);

    // The run the Audit column reports: .izzy/audit-baseline.json, remade Nov 25, 2026 after the triage.
    private static readonly (string On, string Pages, int Findings, int FalsePositives, int DemoPages, int Accepted) Run =
        ("November 25, 2026", "543", 88, 27, 58, 3);

    // Components the screen reader pass has reached, with what was run and how. Everything else is "Not yet".
    private static readonly Dictionary<string, string> ScreenReaders = new()
    {
        ["Switch"] = "VoiceOver with Safari, by script: transcript read, one open question",
        ["LiveRegion"] = "VoiceOver with Safari, by script: one bug found, in Known gaps",
    };

    // Components whose docs examples have accepted findings in the baseline.
    private static readonly Dictionary<string, int> Accepted = new() { ["RichTextEditor"] = 2, ["TextBlock"] = 1 };

    // Components with a gap in the overview's Known gaps.
    private static readonly HashSet<string> Gaps = ["NumberField", "LiveRegion"];

    private static readonly Row[] Meanings =
    [
        new("Keyboard", "\"Keys documented\" links the component's Keyboard navigation table, for keys that IzzyBlazor's script adds. \"No keys of its own\" means the component uses a native element's keys or isn't interactive. The column says where the keys are documented, not that they were tested: the overview describes the keyboard tests."),
        new("Audit", "What izzy audit left open on the component's docs pages, in every example, as they load."),
        new("Screen readers", "The screen readers the component's examples have been run through, and how, or \"Not yet\"."),
    ];

    private List<Status> Rows(string category) =>
        [.. Registry.InCategory(category).Select(c => new Status(
            c.DisplayName, Registry.OverviewHref(c), Api.Component(c.Name)?.Keyboard is { Count: > 0 }, AuditOf(c.Name),
            ScreenReaders.GetValueOrDefault(c.Name, "Not yet")))];

    private static string AuditOf(string name) =>
        "No open findings"
        + (Accepted.TryGetValue(name, out var count) ? $"; {count} accepted in a docs example" : "")
        + (Gaps.Contains(name) ? "; one known gap" : "");
}
